.TH OROC-PUSH-DISTRIBUTOR 1 "" "Oro Runtime 0.2.0" "Oro Runtime Manual"
.SH NAME
oroc\-push\-distributor \- Build or install Oro Push Dev, the Kotlin Android UnifiedPush development distributor.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
oroc push distributor [options]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Build or install Oro Push Dev, the Kotlin Android UnifiedPush development distributor.
.PP
On Linux, '\-\-platform=linux' runs a native Distributor2 service instead of building an APK. It needs a session D\-Bus, an unlocked Secret Service and an ntfy\-compatible server. It runs until interrupted; it does not install or enable a login service. No Node.js, Gradle or Android SDK is required by this Linux distributor command.
.PP
Linux transport uses bounded polling every two seconds, reconnects with two\-to\-thirty second delays and retains ciphertext in an OS\-key\-encrypted journal. Limits are 32 registrations, 32 pending messages of at most 4096 bytes, 1024 boundary\-second deduplication IDs and seven days of pending retention. The encrypted journal has a 256 KiB aggregate limit. Storage or deduplication\-window saturation pauses intake. An acknowledged connector message means durable local receipt, not completed application work.
.PP
Linux HTTPS uses system trust, or only the explicitly supplied PEM CA database with '\-\-ca\-cert'. Redirects are rejected. '\-\-allow\-loopback\-http' permits literal loopback addresses only; it never permits LAN HTTP or DNS names. Apps must separately opt into '[push] development_allow_loopback_http = true' to accept these development endpoints. Use HTTPS for phone distributors. Neither anonymous topics nor this local transport establish trusted enrollment, server authentication or remote revocation.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-platform=<android|linux>\fR
Android APK build (default), or Linux service
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-loopback\-http\fR
Linux only: allow literal loopback HTTP
.TP
\fB\-\-android\-home=<path>\fR
Android SDK (defaults to ANDROID_HOME or ANDROID_SDK_ROOT)
.TP
\fB\-\-gradle\-home=<path>\fR
Gradle 9 installation (defaults to GRADLE_HOME)
.TP
\fB\-\-java\-home=<path>\fR
JDK installation (defaults to JAVA_HOME)
.TP
\fB\-\-data\-dir=<path>\fR
writable build state and persistent development signing key
.TP
\fB\-\-output=<path>\fR
APK destination (default: data directory/oro\-push\-dev.apk)
.TP
\fB\-\-server=<origin>\fR
prefill an HTTPS server in the app; user taps Save and start
.TP
\fB\-\-ca\-cert=<path>\fR
embed a development PEM CA, trusted only by this APK
.TP
\fB\-\-offline\fR
build with cached Gradle/Android dependencies only
.TP
\fB\-\-install\fR
install APK and open its settings on the selected device
.TP
\fB\-\-device=<serial>\fR
explicit adb serial for installation (or ANDROID_SERIAL)
.TP
\fB\-h, \-\-help\fR
show help
.SH NOTES
.PP
Requires Node.js 22+, JDK 17+, Gradle 9, an installed Android platform 34 or newer, and build tools.
.PP
Run 'oroc setup \-\-platform=android' to provision the normal Android toolchain first.
.PP
Uses the runtime's Android Gradle plugin with built\-in Kotlin support. First build may download Android/Kotlin dependencies; \-\-offline requires those dependencies to be cached.
.PP
A debug signing key is retained outside the SDK so updates preserve the app's state.
.PP
Oro Push Dev is a separate sideloaded development APK. Your app still registers its native push handler.
.PP
It connects to 'push server' or an ntfy\-compatible HTTPS server and implements UnifiedPush registration, selection, unregistration and acknowledged delivery. Existing ntfy distributors continue to work with the same server and oro:push APIs; apps choose one distributor.
.PP
The user must enable its visible foreground connection in its settings. Optional CA trust is confined to this APK; hostname verification and Oro's HTTPS endpoint requirement remain.
.PP
Limits: 32 registrations, 256 pending ciphertexts (4096 bytes each), 512 recent ACK identities.
.PP
Unacknowledged ciphertext persists across process loss; a full inbox pauses further intake.
.PP
Reboot resumes an enabled service when Android permits it. Force\-stop requires reopening it.
.PP
Doze, network reachability and Android background policy still require device qualification.
.PP
This is for public development data, not production server authentication or trusted enrollment.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
\fBoroc push distributor \-\-platform=linux \-\-server=http://127.0.0.1:8787 \-\-allow\-loopback\-http\fR
run alongside a local 'push server'
.TP
\fBoroc push distributor \-\-offline\fR
build from the cached Android/Kotlin toolchain
.TP
\fBoroc push distributor \-\-server=https://push.dev.example:8787 \-\-ca\-cert=./dev\-ca.pem \-\-install \-\-device=emulator\-5554\fR
build, install, then review and start Oro Push Dev
.SH SEE ALSO
oroc(1), oroc\-push(1)
